## What I think agents look like in a year

I don't think **every** agent will be persistent; stateless workers are useful and Letta itself explicitly supports one-off stateless sessions. ([Letta][1])

But for serious repeated work, restarting from absolute zero will increasingly look wasteful.

The commodity might become:

```text
foundation model
```

and the scarce things become:

```text
trusted experience
high-quality context
proven procedures
evaluation infrastructure
organizational memory
real-world outcome history
```

This is exactly why I think starting to record experience **now** matters despite the framework churn.

If GLM disappears, fine.

If Letta gets superseded, fine.

If Hydra gets replaced, fine.

The valuable historical object remains:

```text
THIS TASK
    ↓
THIS WORKER STATE
    ↓
THIS CONTEXT
    ↓
THIS PROCEDURE
    ↓
THIS OUTPUT
    ↓
THIS REVIEW
    ↓
THIS REAL OUTCOME
```

Accumulate ten million of those and you have something profoundly useful.

The part I'd build immediately after the first School prototype is actually **Briefings + Peer Review**. They're lightweight, immediately useful on real jobs, easy to price with x402, generate fantastic evaluation data, and naturally feed both the Lab and Schools.

Then you get a beautiful continuum:

```text
I don't know enough
      ↓
BUY BRIEFING

I can do it but want confidence
      ↓
BUY REVIEW

I repeatedly lack this capability
      ↓
ATTEND SCHOOL

This is valuable ongoing demand
      ↓
SPAWN SPECIALIST WORKER

Many workers learn from it
      ↓
LAB DEVELOPS MOAT
```

That is starting to look less like “an agent marketplace” and more like **the education, intelligence, labor and professional-services infrastructure for machine workers**.

